1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,462
Welcome to practicals three and four.

2
00:00:02,462 --> 00:00:10,848
The goal is to learn to spot data types and structure in your own dataset, by asking five simple questions about every column.

3
00:00:11,648 --> 00:00:22,448
The five questions are about the level of measurement, discrete versus continuous, structured versus semi-structured, text data basics, and time series behaviour.

4
00:00:23,248 --> 00:00:24,029
First ask:

5
00:00:24,029 --> 00:00:30,354
are the values labels or numbers, and can you rank them or measure real distance?

6
00:00:30,354 --> 00:00:30,978
Nominal:

7
00:00:30,978 --> 00:00:34,883
categories with no order, like city or blood type.

8
00:00:34,883 --> 00:00:35,507
Ordinal:

9
00:00:35,507 --> 00:00:40,505
categories that can be ranked, like satisfaction or star rating.

10
00:00:40,505 --> 00:00:41,207
Interval:

11
00:00:41,207 --> 00:00:45,893
equal spacing but no true zero, like temperature in Celsius.

12
00:00:45,893 --> 00:00:46,361
Ratio:

13
00:00:46,361 --> 00:00:50,968
equal spacing with a true zero, like height, age or income.

14
00:00:51,768 --> 00:00:54,373
Here is a quick test for any column.

15
00:00:54,373 --> 00:00:59,149
If the values are words, ask whether the categories can be ranked:

16
00:00:59,149 --> 00:01:02,551
no order means nominal, an order means ordinal.

17
00:01:02,551 --> 00:01:07,037
If the values are numbers, ask whether zero means none at all.

18
00:01:07,037 --> 00:01:10,873
No true zero means interval; a true zero means ratio.

19
00:01:10,873 --> 00:01:20,208
Zero degrees Celsius does not mean no temperature, so temperature is interval, while zero income means no income, so it is ratio.

20
00:01:21,008 --> 00:01:22,279
Both are numeric.

21
00:01:22,279 --> 00:01:27,212
The test is whether you can count the values or must measure them.

22
00:01:27,212 --> 00:01:34,313
If you can list every possible value, one, two, three, with nothing in between, it is discrete:

23
00:01:34,313 --> 00:01:38,199
number of children, orders, clicks, defective items.

24
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:43,506
If the value could fall anywhere between two numbers, it is continuous:

25
00:01:43,506 --> 00:01:46,496
height, temperature, time taken, weight.

26
00:01:47,296 --> 00:01:52,636
Look at how the file itself is laid out, not just the values inside it.

27
00:01:52,636 --> 00:01:59,554
A C S V file, an Excel sheet or a database table is structured, with fixed rows and columns.

28
00:01:59,554 --> 00:02:09,256
A J S O N or X M L file, a log file or an email has keys or tags but no fixed table shape, and fields can differ between records.

29
00:02:10,056 --> 00:02:15,622
Free-form text columns need to be broken down before you can analyse them.

30
00:02:15,622 --> 00:02:19,384
Raw text is a full sentence or comment as written.

31
00:02:19,384 --> 00:02:22,618
Tokens are the individual words or symbols.

32
00:02:22,618 --> 00:02:24,724
Text is organised in layers:

33
00:02:24,724 --> 00:02:28,260
character, word, sentence, paragraph, document.

34
00:02:28,260 --> 00:02:33,901
And open-ended answers differ from fixed-label text columns like yes or no.

35
00:02:33,901 --> 00:02:42,552
For example, the sentence I loved this product becomes the tokens I, loved, this, product, and an exclamation mark.

36
00:02:43,352 --> 00:02:49,582
If your data has a date or time column, check whether values move over time in two ways.

37
00:02:49,582 --> 00:02:54,608
Trend is the overall direction in the long run, ignoring short wiggles.

38
00:02:54,608 --> 00:03:01,403
Seasonality is a pattern that repeats at fixed intervals, caused by seasons, holidays or habits.

39
00:03:01,403 --> 00:03:03,173
Plot values against date:

40
00:03:03,173 --> 00:03:08,624
a rising or falling line is a trend, regular repeating bumps are seasonality.

41
00:03:09,424 --> 00:03:10,891
Your cheat sheet.

42
00:03:10,891 --> 00:03:12,359
Words or numbers:

43
00:03:12,359 --> 00:03:14,690
categorical versus numeric.

44
00:03:14,690 --> 00:03:16,848
Can categories be ranked:

45
00:03:16,848 --> 00:03:18,833
nominal versus ordinal.

46
00:03:18,833 --> 00:03:20,559
Does zero mean none:

47
00:03:20,559 --> 00:03:22,458
interval versus ratio.

48
00:03:22,458 --> 00:03:24,703
Can you count every value:

49
00:03:24,703 --> 00:03:27,033
discrete versus continuous.

50
00:03:27,033 --> 00:03:29,968
Rows and columns or tags and keys:

51
00:03:29,968 --> 00:03:32,903
structured versus semi-structured.

52
00:03:32,903 --> 00:03:34,716
Open-ended sentences:

53
00:03:34,716 --> 00:03:35,579
text data.

54
00:03:35,579 --> 00:03:37,651
A repeating date column:

55
00:03:37,651 --> 00:03:40,672
time series, trend and seasonality.

56
00:03:40,672 --> 00:03:44,298
Apply these questions to your own dataset.

57
00:03:44,298 --> 00:03:46,024
Thanks for watching.

